@* Authoring help for ASTM instrument configuration, test mapping, and interface rules. *@
@inject BloodBankApiClient Api
@inject IJSRuntime Js

<div id="instrument-help" class="bb-card" style="margin-top:1rem;">
    <div class="bb-card-title">
        <h3>Instrument interface help</h3>
        <button type="button" class="btn btn-secondary btn-sm" @onclick="OnClose">Close</button>
    </div>

    @if (_loading)
    {
        <div class="loading">Loading help...</div>
    }
    else
    {
        <p>
            An instrument interface has four parts. <strong>Connection</strong> is the TCP listen or client
            endpoint. <strong>Configuration</strong> maps ASTM record fields onto data items such as
            <span class="mono">patient_id</span>. <strong>Translations</strong> turn the instrument's test,
            subtest, value, interpretation, and error codes into the LIS's. <strong>Interface rules</strong> rewrite
            parsed values before the result is posted.
        </p>
        <p class="muted">
            Inbound messages are parsed first, then mapped, then rules run, then the result is stored as
            pending verification. Leave the IP address blank to listen as the server. Restart the API after
            changing a listen port.
        </p>

        <h3 id="instrument-help-configuration" style="margin-top:1.2rem;">Configuration</h3>
        <p>
            Configuration is the field map. Each row names a data item the LIS understands and the ASTM
            locator that holds it on the wire. Paths are 1-based and use the form
            <span class="mono">Record.Field</span> or <span class="mono">Record.Field.Component</span>
            — for example <span class="mono">P.3</span>, <span class="mono">O.5.4</span>, or
            <span class="mono">R.4.1</span>. Record letters are H, P, O, R, Q, C, and L.
        </p>
        <p>
            A data item may be mapped only once. Required items must resolve for the message to post a
            result. Unknown keys and malformed paths are rejected on save.
        </p>

        <h4 style="margin-top:.9rem;">Data items</h4>
        <table class="bb">
            <thead><tr><th>Data item</th><th>Default path</th><th>Required</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                @foreach (var item in _catalog)
                {
                    <tr>
                        <td class="mono">@item.Key</td>
                        <td class="mono">@item.DefaultAstmPath</td>
                        <td>@(item.Required ? "Yes" : "")</td>
                        <td>@item.Description</td>
                    </tr>
                }
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <h4 id="instrument-help-shared-field" style="margin-top:1.2rem;">When subtest_result and test_interp share a field</h4>
        <p>
            Blood-bank instruments often put the cell reaction and the assay interpretation in the
            <em>same</em> ASTM value field, usually <span class="mono">R.4.1</span>. Both
            <span class="mono">subtest_result</span> and <span class="mono">test_interp</span> can map
            to that path. Configuration has a setting that decides which R records are assay-level
            interpretation versus analyte results:
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>Analyte code equals the assay code</strong> (Echo default) — when
                <span class="mono">subtest_code</span> matches <span class="mono">test_code</span>,
                the value is stored in <span class="mono">test_interp</span>.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Analyte code is one of these values</strong> — list instrument codes such as
                <span class="mono">INTERP</span> or <span class="mono">RESULT</span> that always mean
                interpretation, even when they do not match the assay name.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Never</strong> — every R record is an analyte result. Use this when interpretation
                is not sent on a shared value field.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            Comparison is case-insensitive. Rules can still rewrite either field after that choice is made
            (<span class="mono">B Pos</span> to <span class="mono">B|Positive</span>, or a numeric
            <span class="mono">80</span> to <span class="mono">4+</span>).
        </p>
        <p class="muted">Echo-style Group message with “analyte equals assay”.</p>
        <pre class="pre">O|1|0651439A||^^^Group|R||||||||||Blood^Patient
R|2|^^^Anti-A|0^0^0|||||F
R|3|^^^Anti-B|4+^4+^90|||||F
R|8|^^^Group|B Pos|||||F

Anti-A  →  subtest_code = Anti-A,  subtest_result = 0
Anti-B  →  subtest_code = Anti-B,  subtest_result = 4+
Group   →  subtest_code = Group (= test_code)  →  test_interp = B Pos</pre>
        <p>
            Map both data items to <span class="mono">R.4.1</span> unless the instrument actually sends
            interpretation in a different field. Do not invent a second path just to keep the rows unique.
        </p>

        <h3 id="instrument-help-mapping" style="margin-top:1.2rem;">Translations</h3>
        <p>
            Each instrument has its own translations, by field. Values are translated after the field map and
            before the rules, so a rule still sees, and can change, the translated value.
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Result test code:</strong> the assay on a result (Group → ABORH). Several instrument codes may
                share one test; when they do, the test ordered on the specimen wins. A code with no row is
                <em>not posted</em>: the message waits in instrument failures.</li>
            <li><strong>Subtest code:</strong> a well or cell (Anti-D1 → Anti-D). <span class="mono">KEEP</span> stores
                it under the instrument's own code; <span class="mono">IGNORE</span> drops it. An analyte with no row
                stops its assay from posting.</li>
            <li><strong>Order test code:</strong> what the LIS asks the instrument to run for a test when it queries
                for a specimen. One code per test. A test with no row is left out of the download and listed.</li>
            <li><strong>Result value</strong> and <strong>Interpretation:</strong> what the instrument reports and the
                LIS value (B Pos → B|Positive). A row can be limited to one test.</li>
            <li><strong>Error or flag code:</strong> the instrument's error code table, which decides each code on its
                own (OCD-066): the code (message error L.3 or result flag R.7), its meaning from the vendor manual, and
                <span class="mono">Hold</span> (not posted, review or release in failures), <span class="mono">Warn</span>
                (posted with the code and meaning beside the result's source), or <span class="mono">Ignore</span>.
                A code not in the table is held and listed so it can be added.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="muted">Typical Echo rows.</p>
        <pre class="pre">Result test code   Group      →  ABORH
Subtest code       Anti-D1    →  Anti-D
Subtest code       Pos Ctrl   →  KEEP
Interpretation     B Pos      →  B|Positive
Error code         N          →  Ignore   (ASTM normal end)</pre>
        <p>
            Values with no translation are listed under Admin › Interface Setup › Translation table ›
            Unmapped values, named by instrument. <strong>Add translation</strong> there opens this tab with the row ready.
        </p>

        <h3 id="instrument-help-rules" style="margin-top:1.2rem;">Interface rules</h3>
        <p>
            Rules run on each parsed R record after the field map and before posting. The condition uses
            the same expression language as order and test rules. Facts are the data items, with or
            without a <span class="mono">message.</span> prefix.
        </p>
        <p>
            The only action is <span class="mono">set('field','value')</span>. Separate several sets with
            semicolons. Field names must be catalog data items.
        </p>
        <table class="bb">
            <thead><tr><th>Example condition</th><th>Example action</th><th>When to use it</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td class="mono">message.subtest_result &gt;= 80</td>
                    <td class="mono">set('subtest_result','4+')</td>
                    <td>Convert a numeric capture to a reaction grade.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="mono">message.test_interp = 'B Pos'</td>
                    <td class="mono">set('test_interp','B|Positive')</td>
                    <td>Normalize an instrument interpretation to the LIS coded value.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="mono">message.subtest_result IN ('?','C','Q')</td>
                    <td class="mono">set('subtest_result','NT'); set('error','W')</td>
                    <td>Turn instrument error tokens into Not Tested.</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
        <h4 style="margin-top:.9rem;">Things that catch people out</h4>
        <ul>
            <li>
                A grade such as <span class="mono">4+</span> is text, not the number 4. Numeric rules
                (<span class="mono">&gt;= 40</span>) match only raw numeric captures such as
                <span class="mono">40</span> or <span class="mono">80</span>.
            </li>
            <li>Text comparison ignores case and surrounding spaces.</li>
            <li>Rules run from the lowest sort order to the highest. <em>Stop on match</em> skips later rules for that record.</li>
            <li>
                <span class="mono">set('test_interp',…)</span> only affects records the interpretation
                setting classifies as assay-level. Setting it on a cell record has no effect on the posted panel.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <div class="flex gap" style="margin-top:1.2rem;">
            <button type="button" class="btn btn-secondary" @onclick="OnClose">Close</button>
        </div>
    }
</div>

@code {
    [Parameter] public IReadOnlyList<InstrumentDataItemDto>? Catalog { get; set; }
    [Parameter] public EventCallback OnClose { get; set; }

    private IReadOnlyList<InstrumentDataItemDto> _catalog = [];
    private bool _loading = true;

    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync()
    {
        if (Catalog is { Count: > 0 })
        {
            _catalog = Catalog;
            _loading = false;
            return;
        }

        var result = await Api.GetAdminInstrumentDataItemsAsync();
        _catalog = result.Value ?? [];
        _loading = false;
    }

    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
    {
        if (!firstRender)
        {
            return;
        }

        try
        {
            await Js.InvokeVoidAsync(
                "eval",
                "document.getElementById('instrument-help')?.scrollIntoView({behavior:'smooth',block:'start'})");
        }
        catch (JSException)
        {
        }
    }
}
